This suite verifies that mgr/volumes' auto-upgrade of pre-v3 (v1 and v2)
subvolumes to the v3 layout is non-disruptive across a Ceph upgrade. The
work is done by qa/tasks/volumes_upgrade.py.

Matrix
======

  0-from     squid | tentacle         release upgraded from
  1-ranks    1 | 2                    max_mds
  3-setup    v1 | v2                  version of the pre-v3 subvolumes
  4-upgrade  mgr-only | full          ceph-mgr first, or everything at once

That's 16 jobs. Clients are fixed (2-client): client.0 and client.2 are
kernel, client.1 is ceph-fuse, client.3 (kernel) mounts the file system
root as the admin mount that inspects the on-disk layout.

The subvolume attribute profiles below are not facets. overrides/
profiles.yaml defines them, and overrides/subvolumes/ assigns them, one
<subvolume>-<profile>.yaml per subvolume. Every job includes all of these,
so each job's description shows which subvolume got which profile, and its
config shows the attributes. Every job creates all the subvolumes, so they
add no jobs, and a rerun gets the same ones.

Subvolumes and attribute profiles
=================================

Each v1/v2 subvolume (other than clones, purges and the subvolumes removed
with retained snapshots, which are plain v2) is created with an attribute
profile. A profile is a list of attributes (see ATTRS in
qa/tasks/volumes_upgrade.py). Every IO subvolume and the clone source must
be assigned one. Other subvolumes can be added (named sv_attr_*, without a
client, snapshots or clones) with a fragment of their own in
overrides/subvolumes/. The task rejects a config that doesn't follow these
rules, or a fragment whose name doesn't match the profile it assigns. As
configured:

  bare         no attributes, as `fs subvolume create` without options
  full         --size, --uid/--gid, --mode, --namespace-isolated,
               --earmark, user metadata, plus a user xattr, an ACL and a
               file count quota (ceph.quota.max_files) set on the data dir
  partial      --size only
  pool_layout  --pool_layout on a second data pool (added to the volume
               during setup), with --namespace-isolated
  group        no attributes of its own, in a subvolume group created with
               --size, --uid/--gid, --mode and --pool_layout (the second
               data pool), from which it inherits the data pool and uid/gid

  subvolume                 profile      client       pre-v3 snapshots
  sv_io_0                   bare         client.0     0
  sv_io_1                   full         client.1     1
  grp_io/sv_io_2            group        client.2     2
  grp_src/sv_clone_src      pool_layout  -            2 (clone source)
  sv_attr_0                 partial      -            0
  grp_attr/sv_attr_1        full         -            0

v2 subvolumes are created through `fs subvolume create`. No release that
can be upgraded from creates v1 subvolumes, so they are fabricated in the
layout ceph-mgr used to create them in (data and v1 snapshots in
<subvolume>/<uuid>, .meta with version 1), with the attributes of their
profile set the way ceph-mgr would set them. The first snapshot of a v1
subvolume is taken directly in its data dir, and the rest through the old
ceph-mgr, which must handle it as v1. A v1 subvolume without snapshots is
not accessed through the old ceph-mgr, which would upgrade it to v2.

Setup, on the old release
=========================

  - Each IO subvolume is mounted by its client with subvolume-scoped
    credentials (`fs subvolume authorize` for v2, the same caps made by hand
    for v1), and a background writer does IO in it for the rest of the job.
    It writes 16KiB files (fsync, rename into place), keeps checksums
    outside CephFS, and periodically re-verifies recent files and does
    mkdir/rmdir, readdir and stat. Any error (e.g., EACCES or ESTALE when
    the data dir moves) fails the job.
  - Snapshots of the IO subvolumes are taken while their clients write, and
    every pre-v3 snapshot gets user metadata.
  - Each IO subvolume (except a v1 one without snapshots) gets another auth
    ID through the old ceph-mgr, with which an extra ceph-fuse session
    mounts its pre-v3 data dir.
  - 5 async clones are held pending until the old ceph-mgr goes away. The
    old ceph-mgr picks them up with a long
    mgr/volumes/snapshot_clone_delay, which is reset afterwards for the new
    ceph-mgr; pause_cloning would cancel them
    (https://tracker.ceph.com/issues/81383). They are v2, as the old
    ceph-mgr creates clones so, and are of:
      - snap0 of the clone source (3 clones, one in a group)
      - the snapshot of sv_io_1, whose client keeps writing to it
      - the snapshot of a v2 subvolume then removed with
        `--retain-snapshots`, its data dir caught mid-purge
  - 3 v2 subvolumes are removed (`fs subvolume rm`). 2 v2 subvolumes with a
    snapshot (one being the source of the last clone above) are removed
    with `--retain-snapshots`. Purging is let run until at least 10% of the
    files are gone, and then paused (mgr/volumes/pause_purging) with files
    still left. The old ceph-mgr upgrades a v1 subvolume to v2 before
    removing it, and v1 subvolumes can't retain snapshots, so these are v2
    regardless.

Before the upgrade, setup checks that:

  - the clones are pending with nothing copied, and their source snapshots
    report has_pending_clones
  - each pre-v3 subvolume's data dir has the attributes of its profile, as
    the file system has them. These are recorded as the baseline: owner,
    mode, ceph.dir.layout.pool and .pool_namespace, ceph.quota.max_bytes
    and .max_files, and all user.* and system.* (POSIX ACL) xattrs,
    including the earmark
  - a v1 subvolume without snapshots is strictly v1 and untouched
  - every other pre-v3 subvolume works through the old ceph-mgr: getpath,
    `fs subvolume info` (attributes as per its profile), snapshot
    ls/info/metadata/getpath, and still in the pre-v3 layout. Its info and
    snapshot info are recorded as the baseline
  - the attributed group's info (uid, gid, mode, data pool, quota) is as
    created, and is recorded
  - `fs subvolume authorized_list` lists the auth IDs authorized so far
  - each subvolume is marked as a subvolume (hard links out of it get
    EXDEV)

Upgrade
=======

mgr-only (4-upgrade/mgr-only.yaml): only ceph-mgr is upgraded, through a
staggered `ceph orch upgrade`, and the upgrade is then stopped. The new
ceph-mgr can't auto-upgrade, as the old MDS doesn't know the
ceph.dir.subvolume.prevpath vxattr. verify_mgr_only checks that:

  - the writers are still making progress
  - every pre-v3 subvolume keeps working as v1/v2 (a v1 one without
    snapshots may get upgraded to v2, as ceph-mgr always did), with its
    data dir attributes, `fs subvolume info` and user metadata unchanged
    from the baseline
  - the group's attributes are unchanged, and authorized_list is unchanged
  - a snapshot taken on the clone source by the new ceph-mgr is in the
    pre-v3 layout and is listed
  - the purges resume and finish, and the purged subvolumes are gone
  - the pending clones finish, with data matching the snapshot and the
    attributes of their source (uid, gid, mode, data pool, pool namespace,
    quota, earmark), and has_pending_clones turns false
  - the subvolumes removed with retained snapshots stay so: v2, listed,
    snapshot-retained, with their snapshots
  - a new clone of a pre-v3 snapshot (snap1 of the clone source) by the new
    ceph-mgr works, with data and attributes as above

The rest of the cluster is then upgraded, and the writers must still be
making progress.

full (4-upgrade/full.yaml): all daemons are upgraded in one go, and the
writers must still be making progress.

After the upgrade (5-verify.yaml, post_upgrade)
===============================================

Auto-upgrade, with clients doing IO:

  - The first subvolume command on each subvolume upgrades it to v3 while
    its client keeps writing without remounting. The writer must make
    progress afterwards.
  - Each v3 subvolume is checked: getpath returns
    <subvolume>/roots/<uuid>/mnt, .meta is a symlink to .meta.<uuid>, the
    meta is version 3 with that path and state complete, the uuid is the
    pre-v3 one, and the pre-v3 data dir is gone.

Attributes:

  - Every pre-v3 subvolume's v3 data dir has exactly the baseline file
    system attributes (owner, mode, layout pool and namespace, both quotas,
    user xattrs, earmark, ACLs).
  - `fs subvolume info` (type, uid, gid, mode, data_pool, pool_namespace,
    bytes_quota, created_at, earmark, and features for v2) matches the old
    ceph-mgr's report, and the user metadata is intact.
  - The group's attributes are unchanged.
  - authorized_list is unchanged.

Subvolume boundary:

  - For each upgraded subvolume, hard-link probes from its data dir to the
    group dir, the subvolume dir and the incarnation dir give the same
    result as for a subvolume created as v3 in the same group.

Snapshots:

  - On every pre-v3 subvolume and every clone, the snapshot listing is
    exactly the snapshots taken so far, each snapshot's info (created_at,
    data_pool) is as the old ceph-mgr reported it, and pre-v3 snapshots
    keep their user metadata.
  - `fs subvolume snapshot getpath` of each pre-v3 snapshot returns where
    the snapshot now is: a v2 one stays at <subvolume>/.snap/<snap>/<uuid>,
    a v1 one moves with the data dir to
    <subvolume>/roots/<uuid>/mnt/.snap/<snap>. The data there matches what
    the snapshot was taken with. Before the upgrade (and with only ceph-mgr
    upgraded), getpath returns the pre-v3 paths. getpath of v3 snapshots is
    left to the functional tests (fs:volumes).
  - A v3 snapshot is then taken on each, and is listed along with the
    pre-v3 ones.

Clones:

  - The clones pending through the upgrade finish (with the full upgrade,
    while their source is auto-upgraded) and are v3. Their data matches
    the snapshot and their attributes match their source.
  - The clone made with only ceph-mgr upgraded is v3 with its data intact.
  - New clones of pre-v3 snapshots work, with data and attributes as
    above: snap0 and snap1 of the clone source (pool_layout), and the last
    snapshot of sv_io_2 (group, so its data pool is the group's). Their
    sources report no pending clones afterwards.

Purges and retained snapshots:

  - The purges finish, and the purged subvolumes are gone.
  - The subvolumes removed with retained snapshots are still listed as
    snapshot-retained with their snapshots. A retained snapshot can be
    cloned again, and removing the last snapshot removes the subvolume
    and drains the trash.

Client data and credentials:

  - Each writer is stopped. It re-verifies every file it wrote, and the
    file count at the v3 path matches what it wrote.
  - Each client is remounted at the v3 path twice, and each time all its
    data must be intact and the subvolume writable:
      - with its pre-upgrade credentials (authorized for the pre-v3 path)
      - with a new auth ID from `fs subvolume authorize`, whose MDS caps
        must be for the v3 path
  - authorized_list then includes the new auth IDs.

Deauthorize and evict:

  - Each extra ceph-fuse session must have survived the upgrade and
    auto-upgrade, still rooted at the pre-v3 path, with the subvolume
    accessible through it.
  - `fs subvolume deauthorize` removes its auth ID from authorized_list,
    and removes caps for both the pre-v3 and v3 paths.
  - `fs subvolume evict` evicts the session.
  - The old key can no longer mount the subvolume.

Not covered
===========

  - v1 and v2 subvolumes in the same job
  - releases older than squid
  - quota enforcement (only that the quota values survive)
  - casesensitive/normalization, fscrypt/enctag, export pins
  - snapshot schedules, NFS exports and mirroring of upgraded subvolumes
  - `fs subvolume snapshot clone --pool_layout`
